{"authored" [{"question" "q-79710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $v_1=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\-1\\end{pmatrix}$ and $v_2=\\begin{pmatrix}-3\\\\2\\end{pmatrix}$ be eigenvectors of a matrix $A$. Form $P=(v_1\\ v_2)$ using these columns in this order, with their displayed scales unchanged. For the differential equation $x'(t)=Ax(t)$, define the new coordinates by $u(t)=Dx(t)$ where $D=P^{-1}$. Find the exact matrix $D$." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-79710/review.json" "file_sha256" "0a43d3dd916c91f0731793a2d47af424eb2423f00d1a0998364f647b3f9fe58e" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-79710/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "6954b3fb33acf6b17ffd36ffb7e81d21cd3f89a0f80fc1d622d248ed3a3e35bf" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-79710/verification.json" "basis" 2241 "rationale" "Specify the coordinate direction u=Dx, exact ordered eigenvector-column matrix P and unchanged eigenvector scales so D=P^-1 is unique; generic decoupling matrices remain nonunique. Raw source wording, uncertain original response, accepted source c, and source history are preserved. Only study prompt and derivation change."} {"question" "q-79710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Use the given eigenvectors as the columns of $P$ in the stated order and with their shown scales:\n\n$$P=(v_1\\ v_2)=\\begin{pmatrix}1&-3\\\\-1&2\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\det P=1\\cdot2-(-3)(-1)=-1.$$\n\nThe requested coordinate map is $u=Dx$ with $D=P^{-1}$. The inverse formula gives\n\n$$D=\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{pmatrix}2&3\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}-2&-3\\\\-1&-1\\end{pmatrix}.$$\n\nBoth $DP$ and $PD$ are the identity matrix, so this is the exact inverse. If $Av_i=\\lambda_i v_i$, then $AP=P\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2)$ and\n\n$$u'=P^{-1}Ax=P^{-1}APu=\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2)u.$$\n\nThe opposite coordinate direction is $x=Pu$, whose matrix is $P$, not $D$. Fixing column order and eigenvector scales is necessary to specify this exact inverse. For example, $-D$ also yields a decoupled system if one instead uses the rescaled coordinates $\\widetilde u=-u$, but it is not $P^{-1}$ for the specified $P$. The original generic phrase “the decoupling matrix” did not state these conventions. The clarified prompt makes the exact inverse unambiguous; the source accepted this inverse as option c." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-79710/review.json" "file_sha256" "0a43d3dd916c91f0731793a2d47af424eb2423f00d1a0998364f647b3f9fe58e" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-79710/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "6954b3fb33acf6b17ffd36ffb7e81d21cd3f89a0f80fc1d622d248ed3a3e35bf" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-79710/verification.json" "basis" 2241 "rationale" "Specify the coordinate direction u=Dx, exact ordered eigenvector-column matrix P and unchanged eigenvector scales so D=P^-1 is unique; generic decoupling matrices remain nonunique. Raw source wording, uncertain original response, accepted source c, and source history are preserved. Only study prompt and derivation change."} {"question" "q-79710" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-79710/review.json" "file_sha256" "0a43d3dd916c91f0731793a2d47af424eb2423f00d1a0998364f647b3f9fe58e" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-79710/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "6954b3fb33acf6b17ffd36ffb7e81d21cd3f89a0f80fc1d622d248ed3a3e35bf" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-79710/verification.json" "basis" 2241 "rationale" "Specify the coordinate direction u=Dx, exact ordered eigenvector-column matrix P and unchanged eigenvector scales so D=P^-1 is unique; generic decoupling matrices remain nonunique. Raw source wording, uncertain original response, accepted source c, and source history are preserved. Only study prompt and derivation change."} {"question" "q-79710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ be eigenvectors of a matrix $A.$ Find the decoupling matrix of the differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067055/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d157f0876225a12ab7915c9b9e93e63a23f0e92616529d677aae3cfcff364c0" "basis" 2240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067055/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we construct the matrix $E$, whose columns are the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\nE=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, the decoupling matrix can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={E}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067055/state.